And I tried it another way, and he said, okay, well, do it that way.

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:45.730
And I started to rehearse the scene again, and I was just kind of standing there grinning.

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:47.810
And you said, Ronnie, what's the matter?

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:51.480
And I said, well, that's the first idea of mine that anybody ever accepted.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:54.390
And you said, well, that's the first idea that was any darn good.

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:55.190
Now let's do the scene.

00:38:57.250 --> 00:39:00.000
Well, you created an environment, you know, as the head of the show.

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:04.990
environment that they really encourage people to bring their ideas with them to

00:39:04.990 --> 00:39:09.690
work you know and and boy when somebody would come up with a good idea you'd laugh

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:13.970
so hard you'd be crying I mean it was it was a very collaborative place it

00:39:13.970 --> 00:39:21.110
wasn't only me it was Sheldon Leonard who created our show and Aaron Rubin who

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:28.130
produced it the the tolerance the patience the common sense the all of

00:39:28.130 --> 00:39:30.000
those qualities were the qualities that

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:36.100
made for a perfect father just as opium ultimately made for the perfect son and the relationship

00:39:36.100 --> 00:39:40.790
between father and son was the most important ingredient in the show for me when people say

00:39:40.790 --> 00:39:47.200
what's your favorite show you know i did 150 myself uh five years we did 30 in those days

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:55.420
30 a season and one i remember in particular was uh one in which uh uh opie accidentally kills

00:39:55.420 --> 00:40:00.000
a bird a mother bird with his slingshot and what's

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:08.480
wonderful study in how a father will deal with a kid who's done something even though it was accidental.

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:36.060
Why, why?

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:47.780
Remember me telling you to be careful with this thing?

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:50.660
You gonna give me a whippet?

00:40:52.890 --> 00:40:57.140
I'm not gonna give you a whippet.

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:09.260
Do you hear that?

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:15.130
That's those young birds chirping for their mama that's never coming back.

00:41:16.680 --> 00:41:19.430
Just listen to that for a while.

00:41:19.430 --> 00:41:30.000
All right, son.

00:41:31.720 --> 00:41:32.930
Whatcha doin'?

00:41:32.930 --> 00:41:33.930
Fixin' some breakfast.

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:35.360
Oh?

00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:38.230
What sort of things you fixin'?

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:39.700
Bugs and worms and things.

00:41:39.700 --> 00:41:40.700
Oh.

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:43.480
Bugs and worms and things, huh?

00:41:43.480 --> 00:41:45.370
Sounds like another recipe out of the newspaper.

00:41:45.370 --> 00:41:46.290
No.

00:41:46.290 --> 00:41:47.480
Little birds.

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:49.260
Thinkin' last night.

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:54.300
Since their mall ain't comin' back, and since it's my fault, and since just bein' sorry

00:41:54.300 --> 00:41:56.350
won't help, I'm gonna take over and feed 'em.

00:41:56.350 --> 00:41:57.910
Well, you are.

00:42:01.370 --> 00:42:02.660
I can't come in right now.

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:05.630
Come in as quick as you can.

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:06.150
Okay.

00:42:06.750 --> 00:42:10.650
Why are you acting so strange?

00:42:11.630 --> 00:42:14.560
Well, the boy's facing up to some awful unusual problems.

00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:17.730
Fact is, Hope has just become a mother.

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:23.490
I did all the right things.

00:42:24.130 --> 00:42:25.730
I hope you can fly away.

00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:28.040
Please fly away.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:29.900
Please.

00:42:38.990 --> 00:42:41.580
You got two to go now.

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:46.780
Your brother.

00:42:46.780 --> 00:42:52.950
Fly.

00:42:52.950 --> 00:42:53.560
Blinken.

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:03.120
Guess I did a good job, huh, Pa?

00:43:03.280 --> 00:43:04.500
You sure did, son.

00:43:08.050 --> 00:43:10.010
Cade sure looks awful empty, don't it, Pa?

00:43:11.050 --> 00:43:12.820
Yes, son, it sure does.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:17.120
Now, don't the trees seem nice and full?

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:34.600
Still to come, a roll call of the show's most memorable character.

00:43:35.620 --> 00:43:40.130
Mayberry was a town filled with wonderfully colorful characters.

00:43:41.090 --> 00:43:45.750
One of them was Howard Spray, the county clerk played by Jack Buxton.

00:43:46.390 --> 00:43:50.420
Yeah, Howard was doing the strings, but once he cut those apron strings

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:52.960
and got out from under his mother's thumb, joined the lodge,

00:43:53.410 --> 00:43:57.620
went fishing with the guys, he became a regular fellow and sort of fit right in.

00:43:58.340 --> 00:44:00.000
There are some of the other wonderful characters.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:00.960
Who lived in Mayberry

00:44:00.960 --> 00:44:04.230
Anita Corso as Helen Crump

00:44:04.230 --> 00:44:08.460
Betty Lynn as Thelma Liu

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:12.600
Al Smith as Otis Campbell

00:44:12.600 --> 00:44:17.250
Hope Summers as Clara Edwards

00:44:17.250 --> 00:44:20.420
Dick Elliott as Mayor Pike

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:23.980
Clint Howard as Leon

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:28.020
Parley Bear as Mayor Roy Stone

00:44:31.210 --> 00:44:50.870
when we started making the old griffith show all we wanted to do was make it as good as

00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:59.160
possible and i guess we must have done something right the show has never been off the air for

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:01.000
Over 30 years.

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:05.860
Again, you won't find our town of Mayberry on a map,

00:45:06.190 --> 00:45:10.460
but it has found a place in our hearts and minds and memories.

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:12.930
You have made that so.

00:45:13.940 --> 00:45:16.260
And for that, I thank you.

00:45:17.630 --> 00:45:21.730
In a moment, Andy will be joined by the cast

00:45:21.730 --> 00:45:25.250
to sing the Mayberry Union High School Anthem.

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:29.320
We'll say goodnight, and this is the way we'd like to do it.

00:45:32.560 --> 00:45:39.580
May and high victory is yours, well, not.

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:42.930
We'll hit the line for points every time.

00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:48.530
The orange and blue will try, try, try, try.

00:45:48.530 --> 00:45:55.710
And when the victory's won, you'll be our favorite son.

00:45:55.710 --> 00:45:59.920
Proud waves your banner in the sky.
